# Track temporary resources by operation

Status: accepted.

Setup-created PostgreSQL instances survived worker and check completion.
Operation prefixes alone did not provide a resource lifecycle.

Record a durable lease with the operation directory, prefix, controller
session, process identity, and cleanup status. Projects register an
idempotent cleanup script before provisioning. Run cleanup after the full
operation. Preserve resources for interrupted work and retry failed cleanup
at completion and recovery boundaries. Scripts verify external ownership.

A setup exit trap removes resources before the worker can use them. A broad
Docker sweep can remove shared services or resources for unfinished work.
The registered hook keeps external resource policy in the project.

Configured startup commands take precedence when retained work resumes.
Integration conflict diagnostics name differing files and retain both the
verified candidate and the local edits.

Validation: make test (55 runtime tests), Go race checks, changed-command
recovery under SIGINT and SIGKILL, a real Docker fixture, and Darwin arm64
compilation. Darwin execution remains untested.
